Q) What is OutOfMemoryException and How do you Approach that Problem to Solve it.
A) OutOfMemoryError occurs when the JVM cannot allocate memory in a specific memory area such as Heap, Metaspace, or Native Thread memory because the required memory area is exhausted.
-> In production, I would first identify the exact OOM type from logs. 
-> For Heap-related OOMs, I would capture a Heap Dump using jmap or -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError, analyze it using Eclipse MAT or JMC, identify the objects consuming the most memory, and trace why they are not being garbage collected.
-> I would also review GC behavior, memory utilization trends, and thread counts to determine the root cause and fix the memory leak.
-> Investigate Recent Deployments , new features and traffic spikes.

Common Types of OutOfMemoryError
--------------------------------
1. Java Heap Space:- Cause: Memory leak, Huge collections and Objects not getting garbage collected.
2. GC Overhead Limit Exceeded:- Cause: GC is spending most of its time cleaning memory, but recovering very little memory.
3. Metaspace:- Cause: Excessive class loading
4. Unable to Create New Native Thread:- Cause: Too many threads created

Production Approach
-------------------
Step 1: Identify Exact OOM Type. First look at logs
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Metaspace
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: GC Overhead Limit Exceeded
java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: unable to create native thread

Step 2: Capture and Analyze Heap Dump using Tools like JVisualVM and Java Mission Control.
-> Capture Heap Dump: Enable -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError
-> This Heap Dump will help us to identity below questions like,
a) Which object occupies most memory?
b) Why is it still reachable?
c) Which collection is continuously growing?

Step 3: Check GC Behaviour
Symptoms:
a) Full GC every few seconds
b) CPU High
c) Memory never comes down
-> This usually indicates "Memory Leak" because objects remain reachable.

