Q) How do we Investigate when any Deadlock Occurs. Which Specific Memory Space needs to be monitored to understand the root cause if any Dead Lock Occurs
A) If a deadlock is suspected in production, I would first take a Thread Dump using jstack rather than focusing on heap memory. 
-> Deadlocks are generally thread-related issues, not memory issues.
-> I would examine threads in BLOCKED, WAITING, or TIMED_WAITING state and look for messages such as "Found one Java-level deadlock".
-> From the thread dump, I can identify,
a) Which thread is holding the lock
b) Which thread is waiting for the lock
c) The exact code location causing the deadlock

Step 1: Take Thread Dump
-> Get the JVM PID: jps -l
-> Generate Thread Dump: jstack 12345 > threaddump.txt

Step 2: Analyze Thread Dump
-> Open dump and search: "Found one Java-level deadlock", "waiting to lock", "locked" and "BLOCKED"
-> With the above search, JVM often directly points to the threads involved in the deadlock and the service/class holding the lock.
Example:
--------
Found one Java-level deadlock:
==============================
Thread-101: State:BLOCKED
waiting to lock <0x123>
at com.example.OrderService.placeOrder(OrderService.java:45)

Thread-102: State:RUNNABLE
locked <0x123>
at com.example.OrderService.cancelOrder(OrderService.java:85)

-> From this we can conclude:
Thread-101 is waiting for lock 0x123
Thread-102 already owns lock 0x123
-> From this we can identify the exact service/class and line number responsible for lock contention or deadlock.

Step 3: Monitor Thread Count
-> Using Jconsole, VisualVM and Java Mission Control (JMC) Check for Live Threads, Peak Threads and Blocked Threads.
-> If blocked threads continuously increase: 20, 40, 60....and so, then we can ensure Threads are Blocked and Causing Deadlock Issue.

