Q) Will @Transactional always rollback for every exception ?
A) By default, Spring rolls back for unchecked RuntimeException and Error. For checked exceptions, we can explicitly configure rollbackFor when rollback is required.
Example:- @Transactional(rollbackFor = SomeCheckedException.class)

Spring Transaction Rollback — 3 Scenarios
-----------------------------------------
1) Checked Exception + @Transactional
-> repo.save() executes and data is saved within the transaction.
-> Checked Exception occurs.
-> No rollback by default, so the transaction commits and data remains in DB.
Example:-
---------
@Transactional
public void saveOrderInternal(Order o) {
    repo.save(o);
    chargePayment(o);
}

chargePayment(Order o) {
    throw new Exception("Checked Exception:- Payment failed"); //Throws Checked Exception
}

2) Checked Exception + rollbackFor
-> repo.save() executes.
-> Checked Exception occurs.
-> @Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class) tells Spring to rollback.
-> Transaction rolls back, so saved data is not persisted.
Example:-
---------
@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
public void saveOrderInternal(Order o) {
    repo.save(o);
    chargePayment(o);
}

chargePayment(Order o) {
    throw new Exception("Checked Exception:- Payment failed"); //Throws Checked Exception
}

3) Checked Exception + rollbackFor + try/catch
-> repo.save() executes.
-> Checked Exception occurs inside try block.
-> Exception is caught by catch, so it does not reach the transactional method boundary.
-> Spring does not automatically rollback, and the transaction can commit.
Example:-
---------
@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)
public void saveOrderInternal(Order o) {
    repo.save(o);
    chargePayment(o);
}

chargePayment(Order o) {
try{
throw new Exception("Checked Exception:- Payment failed"); //Throws Checked Exception
} catch (){
}
}

