Q) Where did you use Singleton Pattern in Project. (Thread Pool)
A) 1 Example is Shared Thread pool. I have used Singleton Design Pattern to configure 1 Shared ExecutorService as a Spring Singleton Bean.
-> Multiple services reuse it, which avoids unnecessary thread creation and allows us to centrally control the thread pool size.

Before — Every service creates its own Thread Pool
public class EmployeeService {

    private ExecutorService executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

    public void processEmployee() {
        executorService.submit(() -> {
            System.out.println("Processing Employee");
        });
    }
}

public class PayrollService {

    private ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);

    public void processPayroll() {
        executor.submit(() ->
            System.out.println("Processing Payroll"));
    }
}
-> EmployeeService → Thread Pool 1 → 5 Threads
-> PayrollService  → Thread Pool 2 → 5 Threads
-> If Every service creates its own thread pool, This can lead to:
- Too many threads
- More memory usage
- More context switching
- Difficult thread-pool management

After — Shared Singleton Thread Pool (Manual Singleton Implementation)

import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
public class ThreadPoolManager {

    // 1. Create only one object
    private static ThreadPoolManager instance;

    // 2. Shared thread pool
    private final ExecutorService executorService;

    // 3. Private constructor
    private ThreadPoolManager() {
        executorService = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
    }

    // 4. Control object creation
    public static synchronized ThreadPoolManager getInstance() {

        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new ThreadPoolManager();
        }

        return instance;
    }

    public ExecutorService getExecutorService() {
        return executorService;
    }
}

public class EmployeeService {

    private ExecutorService executor = ThreadPoolManager.getInstance().getExecutorService();

    public void processEmployee() {
        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Processing Employee"));
    }
}

public class PayrollService {

    private ExecutorService executor = ThreadPoolManager.getInstance().getExecutorService();

    public void processPayroll() {
        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Processing Payroll"));
    }
}
-> All services use the same thread pool.
-> We Made executorService variable as private so that it cannot be accessed directly from EmployeeService.
-> So ThreadPoolManager.getInstance().executorService; // wont work.
-> We provide a public method to access as "getExecutorService()"
-> This is essentially encapsulation: keep the field private and expose only the access you need.

 After — Shared Singleton Thread Pool (Spring Managed)
-> Since you're using Spring Boot, the cleanest approach is to let Spring manage the Singleton.
@Configuration
public class ThreadPoolConfig {

    @Bean
    public ExecutorService executorService() {
        return Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
    }
}
-> Spring's default scope is Singleton, so there is one ExecutorService bean per ApplicationContext.

