Q) How HashMap works internally. What is Red Black Tree ?
A) In HashMap, To Put the Element, we Usually Calculate Index based on hash(key) & n-1.
-> Based on Index, We put the Element Between 1 to 15 Buckets.
-> Each Bucket as a Node holds [hash | key | value | next]
-> When Bucket has Multiple Nodes, we call it as Hash Collision.
-> When HashMap Bucket has multiple Nodes like Below,
Bucket[5] => [N1] → [N2] → [N3] → [N4] → [N5] → ...

-> Too many Nodes in the SAME bucket. Suppose the bucket reaches around 8 nodes
-> Searching can become slower because we may need to check nodes one by one.
-> So, Java can convert the linked-list structure in that bucket into a Red-Black Tree to improve lookup performance.
Bucket[5]

             N4
            /  \
          N2    N6
         / \    / \
        N1 N3  N5 N7
                    \
                     N8
-> Now Bucket[5] contains a tree instead of a linked list.
-> This tree is specifically a Red-Black Tree, which is a self-balancing Binary Search Tree.

