Q) What is the difference between Serializable and Externalizable ?
A) Both are used for Java object serialization.
Example:-
---------
class Employee implements Serializable {
    int id;
    String name;
    String password;
    double salary;
}

Serializable:-
--------------
-> The process of writing the state of an object to a file is called serialization.
-> process of converting an object from java supported form into a file-supported form
-> It uses writeObject() / readObject() internally to Customize Object.

-> When you serialize, Java automatically serializes all serializable fields.
-> In serialization, everything is taken care of by JVM and the programmer doesn't have any control.
-> But suppose we don't want to store password and we also don't want to store some unnecessary data.
-> With Serializable, we can customize using writeObject() / readObject(), but the default mechanism is still available.
-> It Means, Though We can customize Objects using writeObject(), But Again we have to use Java default serialization mechanism.
-> By using this "defaultWriteObject()", we will end up again loosing control over Data.

Externalizable:-
----------------
-> The main advantage of Externalizable over serialization is, everything is taken care of by the programmer and JVM doesn't have any control.
-> Based on our requirements we can save either the total object or part of the object which improves the performance of the system.
-> You explicitly decide: What Part of the Object to be serialized and Saved.
-> Externalizable contains writeExternal(ObjectOutput oo) and readExternal(ObjectInput in) and have to override by class to control the Serialization and Deserialization process.
Example:-
---------
public void writeExternal(ObjectOutput out) throws IOException {
    out.writeInt(id);
    out.writeObject(name);
    // Don't write password
}

