Q) Class Level and Object Level Locking in java
A) In Java, locks can be applied at the object level or class level using synchronized.
Object-level locking:-
-----------------------
-> An object-level lock belongs to a specific object instance.
-> When a thread enters a synchronized instance method, it locks the current object (this).
-> Other threads cannot enter another synchronized instance method on the same object until the lock is released.

Example:-
---------
class Counter {

    public synchronized void increment() {
        System.out.println("Incrementing");
    }

(or)

public void increment() {
        synchronized (this) {
            System.out.println("Incrementing");
        }
    }

Class-level locking:-
--------------------
-> A class-level lock belongs to the Class object, such as Counter.class.
-> A static synchronized method locks the class object.
-> Only 1 thread can execute any class-level synchronized code for that class at a time, regardless of how many instances exist.
-> Only one thread can execute resetAll() at a time because both threads require the same Counter.class lock.

Example:-
---------
class Counter {

    public static synchronized void resetAll() {
        // Locks Counter.class
        System.out.println("Resetting all counters");
    }

(or)

public static void resetAll() {
        synchronized (Counter.class) {
            System.out.println("Resetting all counters");
        }
    }
}

