Q) Single Lock & Double Lock
Single Lock — Object Level:-
---------------------------
-> Locks the current object instance.
-> If multiple threads use the same object, only one can enter.
-> But if there are multiple objects, each object has its own lock, so threads can execute concurrently.
Example:-
---------
synchronized(this) {
    // critical section
}

EmployeeService obj1 = new EmployeeService();
EmployeeService obj2 = new EmployeeService();

-> In the above Single Lock example, if we have multiple objects and multiple threads access them, each object has its own lock. 
-> Therefore, threads using different objects can execute concurrently because synchronized(this) locks only the current object, not the entire class.
-> If we need to ensure that only one thread across all objects/instances can execute the critical section, Single Lock is not enough.
-> This is when we go for Class-Level Lock (synchronized(EmployeeService.class)).

Double Lock — Object + Class Level
-----------------------------------
-> First locks the object, then locks the class-level monitor.
-> Multiple objects may acquire their own object locks, but only one thread can acquire EmployeeService.class at a time.
-> Therefore, the class-level section is protected across all instances of that class.
Example:-
---------
synchronized(this) {								// Object-level lock
    synchronized(EmployeeService.class) {			// Class-level lock
        // critical section
    }
}

