Q) What are the different transaction isolation levels ?
A) Isolation controls how one transaction sees when changes made by another concurrent transaction.

Transaction Isolation Levels
----------------------------
Isolation.DEFAULT
-----------------
-> Uses the default isolation level of the underlying database.
-> Spring does not choose a fixed isolation level; it delegates to the database.
Scenario:-
-----------
-> T1 starts a transaction and reads Employee Salary.
-> Since Isolation.DEFAULT is used, the database's default isolation level decides how T1 sees the data.
-> For example, MySQL/InnoDB → REPEATABLE_READ, while PostgreSQL → READ_COMMITTED.
-> So, the behavior for dirty/non-repeatable/phantom reads depends on the database being used.

Isolation.READ_UNCOMMITTED
---------------------------
-> Dirty Read.
-> Transaction can read data that another transaction has not committed yet.
Scenario:-
-----------
-> When T1 updates Salary from 40k to 50k and Not Committed
-> T2 Sees and Reads Data as 50k.
-> T1 RollBack to 40k. Now T2 Read Data as 50k which was never existed and committed.
-> This We call as Dirty Read.

Isolation.READ_COMMITTED
------------------------
-> Non-Repeatable Read.
-> Only committed data can be read. No Dirty Read.
-> READ_COMMITTED does NOT promise that the data you read at the beginning of your transaction will remain the same until the transaction finishes.
Scenario:-
-----------
-> When T1 Read Salary as 40k.
-> T2 Update Salary to 30k and Commits
-> T1 Reads again, Salary is 30k inside same transaction.
-> This is called: Non-Repeatable Read. Because T1 could not repeat the same read and get the same result.
-> Same transaction, same query, different result.

Isolation.REPEATABLE_READ
--------------------------
-> Phantom Read:- Same query executed twice, and extra rows appear/disappear.
-> Once a row is read, repeated reads within the transaction should return the same committed version, depending on DB implementation.
-> No Dirty Read and Non-Repeatable Read.
Scenario:-
-----------
T1:- SELECT employees WHERE salary > 50000 (10 rows)
T2:- INSERT employee salary = 60000 (COMMIT)
T1:- SELECT employees WHERE salary > 50000 (11 rows)
-> The new row is a phantom.

Isolation.SERIALIZABLE
----------------------
-> Highest standard isolation level.
-> Transactions behave as if they were executed one after another, rather than concurrently.
-> It provides the strongest protection but can reduce concurrency and performance.

