Q) What exactly @transactional annotation on a function causes issue in production If there are suppose 50 req per sccond 
A) @Transactional itself is not a problem. The problem is how long the transaction stays open and how many concurrent requests hold database resources.
Example:-
---------
@Transactional
public void processOrder() {
    // DB Call
    // External API Call
    // More DB Call
}

And we get 50 requests/sec

Problem 1: Database Connections Get Occupied
-> Every transaction typically holds a DB connection from the connection pool.
-> If your pool size is 10 and 50 requests arrive simultaneously, then 40 requests waiting
-> Eventually we face Connection Timeout.
Example:-
---------
-> Req-1 to Req-10 -> Got DB Connections
-> Req-11 to Req-50 -> Waiting
-> Req-1 to Req-10 Commits
-> Req-11 to Req-50 Faces Connection Timeout Issue and Fails.

Problem 2: External API Calls Inside Transaction
-> If externalService.call(); takes 5 seconds
-> Connection occupied, Locks held and Other requests blocked.
Solution:- Compute Business Logic 1st.  and 

Problem 3: Locks Remain Longer
-> On save(), DB may hold row locks until Transaction Commit
-> If many requests update the same data, Since Request-1 holds lock row, Request-2 and Request-3 will have to wait.
-> This will leads to Lock Contention

Solution:-
----------
-> Compute Business Logic 1st.
-> Keep transactional boundaries small.
-> Avoid external API calls inside transactions.
-> Transaction lasts only for DB update.

