Q) Spring Bean Life Cycle

1) Bean Definition Loading:- Spring reads bean metadata from XML configuration, annotations such as @Component, @Service, or Java-based configuration using @Bean.

2) Bean Instantiation:- Spring creates an instance of the bean using its constructor or a factory method.

3) Dependency Injection:- Spring injects the required dependencies into the bean through constructor injection, setter injection, or field injection.

4) Aware Interfaces Processing (Optional):- If the bean implements interfaces such as BeanNameAware, BeanFactoryAware, or ApplicationContextAware, Spring provides the relevant container information to the bean.

5) BeanPostProcessor Before Initialization:- Spring invokes postProcessBeforeInitialization() of registered BeanPostProcessor implementations.

6) Initialization Callbacks:- Spring executes initialization methods, such as:
-> @PostConstruct:- It is used to execute initialization logic automatically after Spring injects all required dependencies into a bean.
-> InitializingBean.afterPropertiesSet():- A callback method invoked by Spring after all bean properties and dependencies have been set.
-> Custom init-method (init()):- A user-defined initialization method configured through XML or @Bean(initMethod = "...") invoked by Spring after dependency injection and before the bean is ready for use.

7) BeanPostProcessor After Initialization:- Spring invokes postProcessAfterInitialization(). At this stage, Spring may create proxies for features such as AOP, transactions, and security.

8) Bean Ready for Use:- The bean is fully initialized and available for use from the Spring container.

9) Bean Destruction:- When the application context is closed, Spring invokes cleanup methods, such as:
-> @PreDestroy
-> DisposableBean.destroy()
-> Custom destroy-method

In short:- Bean Definition Loading → Instantiation → Dependency Injection → @PostConstruct → afterPropertiesSet() → Custom init() method → Bean Ready → Destruction.

