Q) How to handle an exception that occurs in a child thread from the main thread
A) Exception reaches the main thread only if we explicitly propagate it using mechanisms like Future.get() or CompletableFuture.get()/join().
Approach 1:-
------------
-> try-catch inside Child Thread
-> Child thread handles its own exception.
-> Main thread is not aware of the exception.

Approach 2:- UncaughtExceptionHandler
--------------------------------------
-> Captures uncaught exceptions from child threads.
-> Useful for centralized logging and monitoring.
-> Main thread is not aware of the exception.
Example:-
---------
Thread t = new Thread(() -> {
    int x = 10 / 0;
});

t.setUncaughtExceptionHandler((thread, ex) -> {
    System.out.println("Exception: " + ex.getMessage());
});
t.start();

Approach 3:- ExecutorService + Future.get() (Recommended)
----------------------------------------------------------
-> Child thread exception is captured by the Executor Framework.
-> Future.get() rethrows it as an ExecutionException to the main thread.
-> Main thread can handle the exception using try-catch.
-> If not handled, the main thread may terminate.

Approach 4:- CompletableFuture
-------------------------------
-> Exception can be handled using exceptionally(), handle(), or whenComplete().
-> Calling get() or join() propagates the exception to the calling thread.
-> Suitable for asynchronous and non-blocking programming.

