Q) What is a Cascading Failure in a Distributed System?
A) A cascading failure occurs when a problem in one component or service spreads to other dependent components, causing a growing portion of the system to fail.

Common Causes of Cascading Failures
------------------------------------
-> Dependency failures:- Database overload and A third-party API outage
-> Synchronous dependency chains:- Client → API Gateway → Service A → Service B → Service C → Database. If any service is slow, entire request can fail.
-> Resource exhaustion:- Thread pools, Connection pools, CPU and Memory. Once resources are exhausted, even healthy endpoints may stop working.
-> excessive timeouts:- With Excessive Timeouts, requests may wait indefinitely for a dependency.

Preventing and Mitigating Cascading Failures in Microservices
--------------------------------------------------------------
1) Use timeouts everywhere:- Connection timeout and Read Timeout.
2) Use circuit breakers:- A circuit breaker stops calls to a dependency that is repeatedly failing or responding too slowly.
3) Isolate resources with bulkheads:- Isolate Application Resources like Threadpool and DB Connection Pool.

