Q) Explain Retry Mechanism and Exponential Backoff. 
1) Retry Mechanism:-
--------------------
-> A retry mechanism means trying a failed request again after a temporary failure, such as a network timeout or a service being briefly unavailable.
Example:- Machine Service sends a request to the Alerts MS to create an alert. If Alerts MS returns 503 Service Unavailable, Machine Service can retry the request.

Simple Retry Strategy
---------------------
-> A simple retry retries immediately, for example:- Request failed → retry immediately → retry immediately → retry immediately.

Problem with Simple Retry
-------------------------
-> Retry storm:- Many Machine Service instances may retry together, overwhelming Alerts MS.
-> Thundering Herd Problem:- Many clients retry/request simultaneously, causing a traffic spike and system overload.
-> Increases load:- If Alerts MS is already overloaded, immediate retries send even more requests.
-> Delays recovery:- Alerts MS has less capacity to recover because it keeps processing repeated failed requests.

2) Exponential Backoff:-
------------------------
-> Exponential backoff increases the waiting time between each retry.
-> Example for Machine Service calling Alerts MS:
1st retry after 1 second
2nd retry after 2 seconds
3rd retry after 4 seconds
4th retry after 8 seconds

How it improves stability
--------------------------
-> Reduces pressure: Alerts MS gets time to recover instead of receiving immediate repeated requests.
-> Avoids resource exhaustion: Machine Service does not continuously consume connections, threads, or CPU retrying.
-> Improves success chance: A temporary problem may resolve before later retries occurs.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q) What is jitter, and why is it important ?
A) Jitter is a small random delay added to the retry wait time.

Advantages of Jitter
---------------------
-> All Machine Service instances do not hit Alerts MS at the same moment.
-> It prevents a sudden traffic spike when Alerts MS starts recovering.

Example:-
---------
-> Spring Retry (@Retryable):- Add dependency: spring-retry and spring-boot-starter-aop
-> Use @EnableRetry on Main Class(@SpringBootApplication) or Configuration Class.

@GetMapping("/{machineId}/alert-details")
@Retryable( retryFor = { ResourceAccessException.class, HttpServerErrorException.class },
        maxAttempts = 4, backoff = @Backoff(
            delay = 1000,        // First retry base delay: 1 second
            multiplier = 2,      // 1s → 2s → 4s
            maxDelay = 8000,     // Maximum wait: 8 seconds
            random = true        // Adds jitter
        )
    )

@Recover
 public String recover(Exception ex) {
     return "Fallback Response";
}
-> @Recover is the recovery/fallback method that Spring Retry invokes after all configured retry attempts are exhausted.

Retry behavior
---------------
-> If Alerts MS fails:
Attempt 1: Immediate call
Retry 1: Around 1 second
Retry 2: Around 2 seconds
Retry 3: Around 4 seconds

-> random = true enables jitter. 
-> So, instead of every Machine MS instance retrying at exactly 1, 2, and 4 seconds, each instance waits for a slightly different duration.
For example:
Machine instance A retries near 1.2 seconds
Machine instance B retries near 1.7 seconds
Machine instance C retries near 2.0 seconds
-> This prevents all Machine MS instances from hitting Alerts MS at the same time during recovery.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Q) While Calling External API Call, What Retry Strategy Would You Design? 
External API Retry Strategy:-
-----------------------------
-> Short timeout
