Q) What Observability Tools Have You Used in Production to Troubleshoot
-> In production, I have used a combination of logs, metrics, and distributed tracing to troubleshoot incidents effectively.

Logs:- Tools such as ELK/EFK Stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash/Fluentd, Kibana), Splunk, Datadog Logs, and CloudWatch Logs.
-> Logs tell you why the problem happened.
-> I use structured logs with fields like request ID, user ID, service name, environment, error code, and timestamp.
-> This helps identify exceptions, failed requests, application errors, and unusual behavior.
-> Logs Provide detailed event-level information What exactly failed?, Which user or request was affected? What was the error message or stack trace?


Metrics:- Tools such as Prometheus with Grafana, Datadog, New Relic, CloudWatch, and Azure Monitor.
-> Metrics help me detect and measure the impact of an issue.
-> I monitor CPU and memory usage, disk utilization, pod/container health, request rate, error rate, response latency and database connections
-> Metrics show behavior over time. For example, a Grafana dashboard can show that memory has been gradually increasing for six hours, indicating a possible memory leak.

Dashboards Metrics i Check First are:-
--------------------------------------
Service Overview:- Request rate and Error rate.
Application or API Dashboard:- Affected endpoints, Status-code distribution: 2xx, 4xx, 5xx and Exception and timeout counts.
Kubernetes Dashboard:- Pod status and Node health.

Distributed Tracing:- Tools such as Jaeger, Zipkin and OpenTelemetry.
-> To investigate a single customer request, We can Use Logs.
-> This becomes difficult when Many Microservices are Running and Each Services run in Multiple Instances.

-> Distributed traces tell you where the problem happened across services.
-> distributed tracing tells you the complete request journey and where the failure or delay happened across multiple services.
-> A distributed trace connects the same request across all involved components using a trace ID and span IDs.

