Q) How to Create Custome Annotations
A) By using "@interface" keyword and declare standard meta annotations like @Target and @Retention.
Example:-
---------
@Target(ElementType.Method)
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
public @interface LogExecutionTime{ }

@Target (Meta Annotations)
--------------------------
-> It defines where the annotation can be used.
-> It accepts an array of ElementType
-> It can be applied on various Types like below Example,

ElementType.METHOD:- On Method Level. @LogExecutionTime, @Transactional, @GetMapping
ElementType.FIELD:-	On Class Variables. @ValidCurrency, @NotNull, @Autowired
ElementType.TYPE:- On classes, Interfaces, and Enums. @RestController, @Entity, @Configuration
ElementType.PARAMETER:- On Method parameters. @RequestBody, @PathVariable, @RequestParam
ElementType.CONSTRUCTOR:- On constructors. @Autowired (on constructors)

@Retention (Meta Annotations)
-----------------------------
-> Jave provides 3 retention policies

1. RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME:- Annotation is available at runtime. Use Case: @Autowired, @Transactional and @Component (Spring reads them at runtime).
2. RetentionPolicy.CLASS (Default):- Annotation is stored in .class file but not available at runtime. Use Case: Used by bytecode tools/compilers for processing class metadata.
3. RetentionPolicy.SOURCE:- Annotation exists only in source code and is discarded during compilation. Use Case: @Override, @Lombok and @Data. (used only by compiler).

